(>> Solution to Question 7:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Recursion Fundamentals in Python: Base Cases, Recursive Steps, Call Stack Mechanics, and Stack Frame Activation Records.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Foundations of Recursion:) Tj
T*
(   Recursion is an algorithmic paradigm where a problem is solved by reducing it to smaller) Tj
T*
(   instances of the exact same problem until a trivial, directly solvable instance \(the Base Case\)) Tj
T*
(   is reached.) Tj
T*
(   Two Mandatory Components:) Tj
T*
(   1. Base Case: The terminal stopping condition where the function returns directly without) Tj
T*
(   making further recursive calls.) Tj
T*
(   2. Recursive Step \(Inductive Step\): The statement where the function invokes itself with) Tj
T*
(   modified parameters strictly progressing towards the base case.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Call Stack Mechanics and Stack Frames:) Tj
T*
(   Every time a function is called in Python:) Tj
T*
(   1. A new Stack Frame \(PyFrameObject\) is pushed onto the PVM Call Stack.) Tj
T*
(   2. The stack frame allocates local variables, parameter bindings, operand stack, and return) Tj
T*
(   instruction pointer.) Tj
T*
(   3. Execution of the caller pauses until the child frame completes.) Tj
T*
(   4. When the base case returns, stack frames unwind in Last-In-First-Out \(LIFO\) order, returning) Tj
T*
(   results up the chain.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Detailed Memory Model & Execution Trace \(Factorial 3!\):) Tj
T*
(   def factorial_traced\(n: int\) -> int:) Tj
T*
(   indent = " " * \(4 - n\)) Tj
T*
(   print\(f"{indent}-> PUSH stack frame: factorial\({n}\)"\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Base Case) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
