(   [Problem Statement]: Implement Binary Exponentiation \(Fast Power Algorithm\) in Python using both recursive divide-and-conquer and iterative bit manipulation.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Principles of Binary Exponentiation:) Tj
T*
(   Computing x^n naively via repeated multiplication requires O\(n\) multiplications. For n =) Tj
T*
(   1,000,000,000, this is computationally prohibitive.) Tj
T*
(   Binary Exponentiation \(Exponentiation by Squaring\) reduces this to O\(log2 n\):) Tj
T*
(   Recurrence Relation:) Tj
T*
(   - If n == 0: x^0 = 1) Tj
T*
(   - If n is even: x^n = \(x^\(n/2\)\)^2) Tj
T*
(   - If n is odd: x^n = x * \(x^\(\(n-1\)/2\)\)^2) Tj
T*
(   - If n < 0: x^n = \(1 / x\)^\(-n\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Python Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   class FastPower:) Tj
T*
(   @staticmethod) Tj
T*
(   def recursive_pow\(x: float, n: int\) -> float:) Tj
T*
(   if n == 0:) Tj
T*
(   return 1.0) Tj
T*
(   if n < 0:) Tj
T*
(   return 1.0 / FastPower.recursive_pow\(x, -n\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   # Recursive divide and conquer) Tj
T*
(   half = FastPower.recursive_pow\(x, n // 2\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   if n % 2 == 0:) Tj
T*
(   return half * half) Tj
T*
(   else:) Tj
T*
(   return x * half * half) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   @staticmethod) Tj
T*
(   def iterative_bit_pow\(x: float, n: int\) -> float:) Tj
T*
(   if n < 0:) Tj
T*
ET

