(   [Problem Statement]: Implement a Recursive Merge Sort algorithm in Python. Analyze divide-and-conquer splitting, merging, stability, and O\(N log N\) asymptotic complexity.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Theoretical Principles of Merge Sort:) Tj
T*
(   Merge Sort is a quintessential divide-and-conquer comparison sorting algorithm:) Tj
T*
(   1. Divide: If the list has > 1 element, split the array into two equal halves at mid = len\(arr\)) Tj
T*
(   // 2.) Tj
T*
(   2. Conquer: Recursively sort the left half and right half.) Tj
T*
(   3. Combine \(Merge\): Merge the two sorted subarrays into a single sorted array by comparing) Tj
T*
(   elements in two-pointer lockstep.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Stability and Guarantees:) Tj
T*
(   - Stable: Preserves the relative order of duplicate elements \(using <= during comparison\).) Tj
T*
(   - Deterministic: Always runs in O\(N log N\) time regardless of initial data distribution \(best,) Tj
T*
(   average, and worst cases are all O\(N log N\)\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Python Implementation:) Tj
T*
(   def merge_sort\(arr: list\) -> list:) Tj
T*
(   # Base Case: 0 or 1 element is inherently sorted) Tj
T*
(   if len\(arr\) <= 1:) Tj
T*
(   return arr) Tj
T*
ET

